<DocsLayout>
  <h1>Docker Deployment</h1>

  <h2>Quick deploy</h2>

  <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/mitchelljfranklin/mitch-risk
cd mitch-risk
# Edit secrets in docker-compose.yml or create a .env file
docker compose up -d</code></pre>

  <p>The compose file provisions:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>App</strong> — Next.js server (port 3000) with automatic Prisma migration and seeding</li>
    <li><strong>PostgreSQL 17</strong> — database (port 5432) with persistent named volume</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Secrets to change</h2>

  <p>Edit these in <code>docker-compose.yml</code> before deploying:</p>

  <pre><code>{`AUTH_SECRET: "your-long-random-secret-here"
APP_ENCRYPTION_KEY: "your-32-char-minimum-encryption-key"
CRON_SECRET: "your-cron-secret-here"
APP_URL: "https://risk.your-domain.com"`}</code></pre>

  <p>Generate secrets:</p>
  <pre><code>openssl rand -base64 32   # for AUTH_SECRET
openssl rand -base64 32   # for APP_ENCRYPTION_KEY
openssl rand -hex 16      # for CRON_SECRET</code></pre>

  <h2>First-run setup</h2>

  <p>After starting, the seed creates:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Three roles (Admin, Reviewer, Viewer)</li>
    <li>ISO 27001:2022, SOC 2, NIST CSF, Essential Eight frameworks</li>
    <li>Default scoring weights, RAG thresholds, and file limits</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Navigate to <code>https://your-domain.com</code> — you'll be redirected to the setup page to create the admin account.</p>

  <h2>Volumes</h2>

  <table class="key-table">
    <tr>
      <th>Volume</th>
      <th>Purpose</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>db_data</code></td>
      <td>PostgreSQL data (persistent across restarts)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>evidence_data</code></td>
      <td>Uploaded files (evidence, certifications, attachments)</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h2>Upgrading</h2>

  <pre><code>git pull
docker compose up -d --build</code></pre>

  <p>Prisma migrations run automatically on container start. The seed is idempotent (upserts), so system roles and frameworks stay in sync.</p>

  <h2>Multi-container considerations</h2>

  <p>For horizontal scaling, note:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The rate limiter is <strong>in-memory per-process</strong> — sufficient for single-container deployment. For multi-container, a Redis-backed limiter would be needed (not currently implemented).</li>
    <li>File storage using the local-disk provider is tied to the container's filesystem. For multi-container or cloud deployments, switch to S3 or Azure Blob in Settings → Storage.</li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
